# Engineering Security

Always-on baseline. When installed, the `security` rule adds detailed controls (auth, headers, CSRF/SSRF, OWASP Top 10:2025 map) and the `owasp-llm-top10` rule covers LLM features.

## Untrusted input

- Treat all external data as hostile: request bodies, headers, query params, file names, URLs, stored data, and LLM output
- Validate type, format, range, and length at the system boundary; reject invalid input rather than sanitising and proceeding
- Never build queries, commands, or markup by string concatenation; use parameterised queries, argument arrays, and context-aware output encoding

## Secure by default, least privilege

- New endpoints require authentication; public access is an explicit, documented opt-in
- Authenticate first, then authorise at the resource level (verify ownership, not only role); default deny
- Services, tokens, and CI jobs get only the permissions they need; prefer short-lived credentials

## Secrets

- Never put secrets in source, committed config, or logs; inject them at runtime from a secrets manager or workload identity
- Rotate on suspected compromise

## Sensitive data

- Never log passwords, tokens, API keys, private keys, full card numbers/CVV, national IDs, or PII combinations
- Mask at the log sink, not by developer discipline alone
- Collect and retain only what is needed

## Cryptography

- Use vetted platform libraries; never invent algorithms or protocols
- No MD5/SHA-1 for security; hash passwords with Argon2id, scrypt, or bcrypt
- TLS 1.2+ (prefer 1.3) for all transport

## Failures

- Fail closed: an error during an auth, validation, or payment check must deny, not allow
- Return generic errors to callers; never expose stack traces, SQL errors, or internal paths
- Log details server-side with a correlation ID

## Supply chain

- Pin and lock dependencies; scan for known vulnerabilities in CI and fail on high/critical
- Add dependencies deliberately (maintained, reputable, needed); remove unused ones

## Before completion

- [ ] Inputs validated at the boundary; no concatenated queries or commands
- [ ] AuthN and resource-level AuthZ on every non-public entry point
- [ ] No secrets in code, config, or logs; no sensitive data logged
- [ ] Error paths fail closed and reveal no internals
- [ ] New dependencies justified, pinned, and scanned
